This is our

566
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,000
current bootloader's

567
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:54,260
basic design idea.

568
00:16:54,260 --> 00:16:56,060
As you read the code,

569
00:16:56,060 --> 00:16:56,960
keep this concept in mind,

570
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:58,000
and you'll be fine.

571
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,880
Now the question becomes,

572
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,260
how do I figure out

573
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:04,100
whether the current

574
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:06,500
core is the first one?

575
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:09,060
And how to initialize,

576
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:11,380
and how to jump into the C execution environment?

577
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:14,320
Because for the large chunks of code that follow,

578
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,320
we definitely want to write them in C,

579
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:15,920
not in assembly,

580
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,660
since writing it in assembly is too complicated.

581
00:17:17,660 --> 00:17:20,520
Now, the next two questions are actually fairly easy to solve.

582
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:21,740
Because the earlier material,

583
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:22,740
the relevant knowledge,

584
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:24,640
has actually all been covered before.

585
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:25,400
Right, everyone gets that?

586
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,000
But the trickiest part,

587
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:27,660
probably the first one,

588
00:17:27,660 --> 00:17:30,960
is how to determine whether the current hart is the first one — this concept of 'hart'.

589
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:33,880
Let's focus on that question next.

590
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:36,500
That is, how to determine whether the current hart is the first one.

591
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:42,080
Now this involves some concepts that we didn't cover,

592
00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:44,200
when we talked about the RISC-V architecture earlier,

593
00:17:44,200 --> 00:17:45,380
some concepts we haven't discussed.

594
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:48,100
As we mentioned before,

595
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:54,620
besides the 32 general-purpose registers,

596
00:17:54,620 --> 00:17:56,700
that is, x0 through x31,

597
00:17:56,700 --> 00:18:00,800
for each privilege level, in fact,

598
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:03,580
we actually have our own set of specific registers.

599
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:05,800
Right now we have machine,

600
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:06,720
and we have user,

601
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:07,840
What supervisor

602
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:08,460
These modes.

603
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:10,620
Each mode has its own set of registers.

604
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:12,860
This set of registers

605
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:15,940
we call them control registers.

606
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:18,440
As for how we read

607
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:21,160
your hart ID,

608
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:21,820
this number,

609
00:18:21,820 --> 00:18:23,640
it is actually read from these registers.

610
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:24,380
Does everyone understand?

611
00:18:24,380 --> 00:18:25,980
These are some special registers,

612
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:27,660
called CSRs,

613
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:29,080
CSR for short.

614
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:30,420
Now for these registers,

615
00:18:30,420 --> 00:18:30,800
we

616
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:33,180
we have also written it here,

617
00:18:33,180 --> 00:18:33,980
that is, each level

618
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:35,060
has its own set.

619
00:18:35,060 --> 00:18:36,160
And then,

620
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:37,560
at a higher privilege level,

621
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:38,780
it can access the lower level,

622
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:39,900
but not the other way around.

623
00:18:39,900 --> 00:18:40,360
Right?

624
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:41,980
That's easy to understand,

625
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:43,280
since it's about access control.

626
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:44,420
In user mode,

627
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:45,200
of course you cannot access

628
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:46,100
registers of machine mode or

629
00:18:46,100 --> 00:18:47,460
supervisor mode,

630
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:47,780
can you?

631
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:48,960
Just like user mode

632
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:50,340
cannot access kernel mode.

633
00:18:50,340 --> 00:18:50,580
So,

634
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:53,660
how do we access these registers?

635
00:18:53,660 --> 00:18:55,780
Well, there are special instructions.

636
00:18:55,780 --> 00:18:58,760
These are not standard integer operation instructions.

637
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:02,880
They are a type of instruction called the CSR extension instructions.

638
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:04,720
We said that RISC-V's

639
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:07,120
instruction design is modular, right?

640
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:08,940
So for these special requirements,

641
00:19:08,940 --> 00:19:11,260
It defines a set of special instructions

642
00:19:11,260 --> 00:19:12,780
to carry out these operations.

643
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:15,980
When we implement these operations later,

644
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:17,860
we will mostly be dealing with

645
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:21,040
these privileged instructions and privileged registers.

646
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:25,320
So let's first look at

647
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:26,200
Machine mode,

648
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:27,160
because we know that

649
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:28,800
as soon as the power comes on,

650
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:32,360
the CPU runs in Machine mode by default.

651
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:34,860
It's a bit like the real mode in x86.

652
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:39,100
So what we need to focus on

653
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:40,160
is what happens right at power-on,

654
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:41,580
since we are currently in that power-on state.

655
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:42,260
At power-on,

656
00:19:42,260 --> 00:19:44,060
how do we operate in Machine mode

657
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:45,260
to perform the relevant operations?

658
00:19:45,260 --> 00:19:47,060
As for Machine mode,

659
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:47,880
the registers

660
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:48,460
are quite numerous.

661
00:19:48,460 --> 00:19:49,700
You can see that it's listed here

662
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:50,960
in this specific

663
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:52,040
you can go and refer to it

664
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:54,480
Reference 2 is the privileged instructions

665
00:19:54,480 --> 00:19:57,180
chapter 2.4 of that specification

666
00:19:57,180 --> 00:19:59,120
this table in chapter 2

667
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:00,740
I've listed it right here

668
00:20:00,740 --> 00:20:02,060
Now among them

669
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:03,900
look here, there are a bunch of

670
00:20:03,900 --> 00:20:05,540
these Machine Information Registers

671
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:06,420
that is to say,

672
00:20:06,420 --> 00:20:10,200
things about the CPU, like the Hart

673
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:11,320
and some ID values

674
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:12,500
can be read out from

675
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:13,120
these registers

676
00:20:13,120 --> 00:20:16,300
Let's get a basic understanding first

677
00:20:16,300 --> 00:20:18,480
we'll cover the details later

678
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:20,360
So the key point is,

679
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:22,020
how do we read these registers?

680
00:20:22,020 --> 00:20:22,720
As we just mentioned,

681
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:23,740
These registers cannot be accessed

682
00:20:23,740 --> 00:20:24,720
using the ones we talked about earlier,

683
00:20:24,720 --> 00:20:25,540
the arithmetic instructions

684
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:26,920
and so on.

685
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:29,020
We have a set of special instructions

686
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:30,740
for accessing these special registers.

687
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:31,940
So here,

688
00:20:31,940 --> 00:20:33,160
there are just a few of them,

689
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:34,140
like CSR,

690
00:20:34,140 --> 00:20:35,340
RWCSRS.

691
00:20:35,340 --> 00:20:36,540
And correspondingly,

692
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:37,280
there are also some

693
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:39,440
that can be accessed

694
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:40,700
using a bit-manipulation style.

695
00:20:40,700 --> 00:20:42,700
We won't go into

696
00:20:42,700 --> 00:20:43,840
the details here.

697
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:44,860
We mainly focus on

698
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:45,640
the ones we care about.

699
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:46,460
Two of them are especially relevant,

700
00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:47,900
namely these two.

701
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:50,890
These two

702
00:20:50,890 --> 00:20:52,830
These two instructions

703
00:20:52,830 --> 00:20:54,930
Let's first take a look at

704
00:20:54,930 --> 00:20:56,670
the CSRW instruction

705
00:20:56,670 --> 00:20:58,290
This instruction

706
00:20:58,290 --> 00:20:59,550
its full name is

707
00:20:59,550 --> 00:21:02,590
Atomic Read Write CSR

708
00:21:02,590 --> 00:21:05,550
So this RW

709
00:21:05,550 --> 00:21:06,650
you can actually understand it as

710
00:21:06,650 --> 00:21:08,570
the abbreviation for Read Write

711
00:21:08,570 --> 00:21:11,830
And CSR is simply CSR,

712
00:21:11,830 --> 00:21:14,170
Control and Status Register

713
00:21:14,170 --> 00:21:15,650
Now here,

714
00:21:15,650 --> 00:21:17,070
there is also an atomic operation

715
00:21:17,070 --> 00:21:18,590
Atomic refers to the concept of an atomic operation

716
00:21:18,590 --> 00:21:20,530
Let's take a look together at what this means

717
00:21:20,530 --> 00:21:23,170
So this CSRW instruction

718
00:21:23,170 --> 00:21:24,250
how does it actually work?

719
00:21:24,250 --> 00:21:25,790
We have an explanation here

720
00:21:25,790 --> 00:21:27,110
Let's all take a look at it together

721
00:21:27,110 --> 00:21:27,670
That is to say,

722
00:21:27,670 --> 00:21:31,770
it first reads the value in the CSR.

723
00:21:31,770 --> 00:21:33,150
Because this instruction

724
00:21:33,150 --> 00:21:34,230
its specification works like this:

725
00:21:34,230 --> 00:21:35,850
RD CSR RS1

726
00:21:35,850 --> 00:21:38,190
it first takes out the value in here,

727
00:21:38,190 --> 00:21:42,390
and zero-extends it to the width of the XLEN bits.

728
00:21:42,390 --> 00:21:43,970
This is easy to explain.

729
00:21:43,970 --> 00:21:45,830
You've already seen this many times before.

730
00:21:45,830 --> 00:21:46,430
What does zero-extension mean?

731
00:21:46,430 --> 00:21:48,270
It means the missing bits are filled with zeros.

732
00:21:48,270 --> 00:21:49,470
And then,

733
00:21:49,470 --> 00:21:50,410
where is it written to?

734
00:21:50,410 --> 00:21:51,530
It's written into RD.

735
00:21:51,530 --> 00:21:54,010
It's written into RD.

736
00:21:54,010 --> 00:21:57,710
Then the value in RS1 is written into the CSR.

737
00:21:57,710 --> 00:22:02,640
How many steps is this?

738
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:03,960
This is two steps.

739
00:22:03,960 --> 00:22:05,660
That is, the first step is to

740
00:22:05,660 --> 00:22:08,460
move the content over here first.

741
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:10,160
Second step.

742
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:13,340
Move the content over here again.

743
00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:14,140
Everyone gets it, right?

744
00:22:14,140 --> 00:22:15,680
It's very simple—just these two steps.

745
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:17,340
But here's one thing to note.

746
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:19,700
The concept of atomic.

747
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:20,480
That is to say,

748
00:22:20,480 --> 00:22:23,720
these two steps must be atomic.

749
00:22:23,720 --> 00:22:24,180
What does that mean?

750
00:22:24,180 --> 00:22:26,300
It means that these two steps,

751
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:27,280
the so-called atomicity,

752
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:28,860
means they are completed within a single instruction,

753
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:30,460
and cannot be interrupted.

754
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:31,760
This is what makes this instruction

755
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:32,940
slightly special.

756
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:35,100
It actually accomplishes the effect of two steps of

757
00:22:35,100 --> 00:22:36,300
moving registers in one atomic operation.

758
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:39,160
Let me give a simple example.

759
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:42,360
For instance, here is an example,

760
00:22:42,360 --> 00:22:45,000
say there's an example right here.

761
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:46,520
What does this mean?

762
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:49,040
This mscratch,

763
00:22:49,040 --> 00:22:51,900
this is a code name for a CSR,

764
00:22:51,900 --> 00:22:53,220
so it's a kind of

765
00:22:53,220 --> 00:22:55,060
register we'll be using later on,

766
00:22:55,060 --> 00:22:56,280
a CSR register.

767
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:57,820
Now, based on the explanation we just gave,

768
00:22:57,820 --> 00:22:58,860
the effect it achieves is this:

769
00:22:58,860 --> 00:23:01,900
first take the value inside mscratch

770
00:23:01,900 --> 00:23:04,580
and put it into the t6 register,

771
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:05,600
which is a general-purpose register.

772
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:06,840
First do that,

773
00:23:06,840 --> 00:23:08,240
and then take the value of t6

774
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:09,800
and put it back into here.

775
00:23:09,800 --> 00:23:12,260
We can write the equivalent like this.

776
00:23:12,260 --> 00:23:13,200
Now think about it — what effect

777
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:14,360
does this actually achieve?

778
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:17,930
It swaps the values

779
00:23:17,930 --> 00:23:18,750
of the two registers.

780
00:23:18,750 --> 00:23:20,090
Do you all understand what I mean?

781
00:23:20,090 --> 00:23:22,430
But what we need to note is

782
00:23:22,430 --> 00:23:23,490
this swap operation

783
00:23:23,490 --> 00:23:25,030
is completed within a single atomic process

784
00:23:25,030 --> 00:23:26,610
and cannot be interrupted

785
00:23:26,610 --> 00:23:28,230
Its only difference is right here

786
00:23:28,230 --> 00:23:33,190
Now, in the rd position

787
00:23:33,190 --> 00:23:35,330
we can also place x0

788
00:23:35,330 --> 00:23:37,030
We talked about x0 earlier

789
00:23:37,030 --> 00:23:38,690
So if that is the case

790
00:23:38,690 --> 00:23:40,410
then in fact,

791
00:23:40,410 --> 00:23:42,290
the first step we just mentioned

792
00:23:42,290 --> 00:23:44,110
becomes completely meaningless

793
00:23:44,110 --> 00:23:46,190
Sometimes we use this

794
00:23:46,190 --> 00:23:47,010
We will see it later

795
00:23:47,010 --> 00:23:49,990
and when we do, we will go over it again

796
00:23:49,990 --> 00:23:54,520
So this is our basic instruction

797
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:55,860
that is, CSRR

798
00:23:55,860 --> 00:23:57,440
This is our basic instruction

799
00:23:57,440 --> 00:23:59,740
Now, in common situations,

800
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:00,980
here comes another instruction

801
00:24:00,980 --> 00:24:02,400
In the usual case,

802
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:03,680
we use this instruction to implement

803
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:07,660
a write operation to the CSRSR instruction

804
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:09,460
called CSRSR.

805
00:24:09,460 --> 00:24:10,720
This is a pseudo-instruction,

806
00:24:10,720 --> 00:24:13,180
and it is actually equivalent to writing it this way.

807
00:24:13,180 --> 00:24:14,580
Now, think about this form,

808
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:16,460
here it writes to x0.

809
00:24:16,460 --> 00:24:18,440
Based on what we just explained,

810
00:24:18,440 --> 00:24:19,940
how is it actually implemented?

811
00:24:19,940 --> 00:24:22,720
It writes this value over here,

812
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:23,920
that's the first step.

813
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:26,300
Then it brings this value back over,

814
00:24:26,300 --> 00:24:27,400
that's the second step.

815
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:28,400
Right?

816
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:30,240
Now, as we covered just now,

817
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:32,040
if rd is x0,

818
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:34,460
the first step is actually meaningless.

819
00:24:34,460 --> 00:24:35,480
You see what I mean?

820
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:37,480
So this first step

821
00:24:37,480 --> 00:24:39,460
Actually, there won't be any

822
00:24:39,460 --> 00:24:40,880
any activity at all

823
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:43,640
In fact, at the hardware level, it means that

824
00:24:43,640 --> 00:24:44,740
it simply doesn't consume power

825
00:24:44,740 --> 00:24:45,700
Put simply,

826
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:47,920
in this situation, it won't

827
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:49,200
it won't perform any action at all

828
00:24:49,200 --> 00:24:51,700
It will only perform the second step,

829
00:24:51,700 --> 00:24:53,940
writing RS into this

830
00:24:53,940 --> 00:24:55,120
Now think about this effect:

831
00:24:55,120 --> 00:24:56,980
isn't it essentially equivalent to

832
00:24:56,980 --> 00:24:58,900
performing a write operation on this CSR, this special register

833
00:24:58,900 --> 00:24:59,960
performing a write operation?

834
00:24:59,960 --> 00:25:00,540
Don't you think so?

835
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:02,840
So this instruction

836
00:25:02,840 --> 00:25:04,240
is actually what we frequently use

837
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:06,760
this command to write to a CSR register

838
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:11,100
This is what I want to

839
00:25:11,100 --> 00:25:12,420
highlight for you as a key point

840
00:25:12,420 --> 00:25:13,420
Of course, this instruction

841
00:25:13,420 --> 00:25:14,440
It also has other uses,

842
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:15,700
and when we come to use it later,

843
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:16,320
we'll take another look.

844
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:18,680
So this is CSR RW,

845
00:25:18,680 --> 00:25:20,560
and then there is another instruction

846
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:22,140
called CSRS.

847
00:25:22,140 --> 00:25:23,720
Now, this one

848
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:27,540
is called Read and Set Bits in the CSR.

849
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:29,780
So let's take a look at the idea behind it.

850
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:30,860
What it means is,

851
00:25:30,860 --> 00:25:33,340
it first reads out the value in the CSR,

852
00:25:33,340 --> 00:25:35,240
and after extending it,

853
00:25:35,240 --> 00:25:35,760
it writes it into rd.

854
00:25:35,760 --> 00:25:37,280
In other words, there's nothing special here;

855
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:38,100
that's just how it works.

856
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:39,180
The first step does exactly this.

857
00:25:39,180 --> 00:25:41,220
It's essentially just doing some zero-extension,

858
00:25:41,220 --> 00:25:42,900
which is not our main focus here.

859
00:25:42,900 --> 00:25:43,980
And then,

860
00:25:43,980 --> 00:25:44,980
however,

861
00:25:44,980 --> 00:25:46,420
For the next step,

862
00:25:46,420 --> 00:25:48,220
it is different from the previous command.

863
00:25:48,220 --> 00:25:51,340
It checks the bits of RC one by one,

864
00:25:51,340 --> 00:25:54,940
and if a certain bit is 1,

865
00:25:54,940 --> 00:25:56,940
it sets the corresponding bit in RC to 1.

866
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:57,860
That is to say,

867
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:00,340
it embodies this idea of setting a bit.

868
00:26:00,340 --> 00:26:01,880
What does that mean?

869
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:03,740
You can think of it this way:

870
00:26:03,740 --> 00:26:04,560
this part here,

871
00:26:04,560 --> 00:26:05,700
it is essentially a,

872
00:26:05,700 --> 00:26:08,420
it has the effect of a mask.

873
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:09,880
I don't know if you have encountered

874
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:12,000
this kind of thing in C,

875
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:13,480
the concept of a mask.

876
00:26:13,480 --> 00:26:16,440
When we run commands,

877
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:17,240
there is this kind of concept,

878
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:18,420
that is, when you change the mode,

879
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:20,400
like 777, 666, 664,

880
00:26:20,400 --> 00:26:21,660
you remember that mask concept, right?

881
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:23,240
There's a command called MUSK, right?

882
00:26:23,240 --> 00:26:26,300
So it's basically like

883
00:26:26,300 --> 00:26:27,460
let me explain it here

884
00:26:27,460 --> 00:26:28,940
Basically, this is doing a mask operation

885
00:26:28,940 --> 00:26:29,940
What does that mean?

886
00:26:29,940 --> 00:26:32,420
It means that if a certain bit in this is 1,

887
00:26:32,420 --> 00:26:34,780
then that bit will actually become 1,

888
00:26:34,780 --> 00:26:36,500
meaning a certain bit in this register becomes 1.

889
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:37,460
If it's 0,

890
00:26:37,460 --> 00:26:39,740
that bit actually stays unchanged.

891
00:26:39,740 --> 00:26:42,260
So that's the effect it has.

892
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:44,380
Also, let me emphasize again:

893
00:26:44,380 --> 00:26:47,240
these two steps are still carried out atomically.

894
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:50,620
Now, later on,

895
00:26:50,620 --> 00:26:52,200
in our exercises,

896
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:53,780
we actually won't focus much on

897
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:54,860
this setting of bits.

898
00:26:54,860 --> 00:26:57,260
When we use the CSRS command,

899
00:26:57,260 --> 00:26:58,640
what we mostly want

900
00:26:58,640 --> 00:26:59,540
is to use this command

901
00:26:59,540 --> 00:27:01,440
Now, for CSR registers,

902
00:27:01,440 --> 00:27:02,660
to perform a read operation,

903
00:27:02,660 --> 00:27:05,400
let's take a look.

904
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:07,840
We actually have this

905
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:08,640
instruction

906
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:10,360
called CSRR.

907
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:12,500
The R stands for read,

908
00:27:12,500 --> 00:27:14,220
which is just the opposite

909
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:15,380
of the write one we saw earlier.

910
00:27:15,380 --> 00:27:16,900
Now, as we explained earlier,

911
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:18,100
it is actually equivalent to

912
00:27:18,100 --> 00:27:19,700
